# !! DO NOT TYPE A REAL VALUE INTO THIS FILE. It is committed and this repository is
# !! PUBLIC. The placeholders below are deliberately not blank, so that a value left
# !! unreplaced fails loudly at authentication instead of silently becoming an empty
# !! credential. Copy this file first; edit only the copy.
#
# TEMPLATE — committed. Copy it, do not edit it in place:
#
#   cp env.secret.stalwart-bootstrap.example .env.secret.stalwart-bootstrap
#   $EDITOR .env.secret.stalwart-bootstrap
#   ../../../scripts/encrypt-env-files.sh apps/production/email-stalwart
#
# Run the encrypt step YOURSELF. The agent must never create or read the real file — see
# env.secret.stalwart-cli.example for what happened the one time it did.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only used to bootstrap a FRESH server, by `./scripts/stalwart-apply bootstrap`, which
# applies stalwart-config/accounts.ndjson.
#
# There is a chicken-and-egg problem at first boot. stalwart-cli normally authenticates
# with an API key, but an API key is a credential attached to an account, and a fresh
# server has no accounts. The recovery admin is the way out: it bypasses the database
# entirely, so it can authenticate before any account exists. The bootstrap job therefore
# uses basic auth rather than a bearer token.
#
# These two values are the SAME credential already held as STALWART_RECOVERY_ADMIN in
# .env.secret.stalwart, which is stored as a single `username:password` pair. stalwart-cli
# wants them separately, hence the split.
#
#   STALWART_RECOVERY_ADMIN=admin:s0mep4ssword
#     ->  STALWART_USER=admin
#     ->  STALWART_PASSWORD=s0mep4ssword
#
# Keep them in sync. If the recovery admin is ever rotated, rotate this too, or the next
# rebuild will fail at the first step.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AFTER THE REBUILD
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. The bootstrap apply creates the `admin` and `automation` accounts with no passwords.
# 2. Set both passwords in the admin UI, and mint a new API key on `admin` (API key
#    secrets are server-generated and cannot be declared, so the old one is unrecoverable).
# 3. Put that key in .env.secret.stalwart-cli, encrypt, and the normal
#    `./scripts/stalwart-apply apply` takes over for all configuration.
#
# Upstream advises removing the recovery admin from the environment once the work needing
# it is done. Ours is set permanently on the StatefulSet as a break-glass credential, which
# is a deliberate departure — it is the only way back in if the admin account is lost.
#
# No quotes, no trailing spaces — everything after `=` is the value.

STALWART_USER=<REPLACE-ME>
STALWART_PASSWORD=<REPLACE-ME>
